            UsapaBasics),
        D(87, "Serve Depth Ladder", "Serving", 2.5m, 10, Solo, Bucket + ", " + Cones,
            "Climb a depth ladder so your serve lands deep on purpose, not by accident.",
            "Place a cone at mid-box, another at the back third line, and a third one foot inside the baseline of one service box. Bucket of balls behind the baseline.",
            "Serve 5 balls past the first cone, then 5 past the second, then 5 past the third without going long. Miss a target and repeat that rung. Switch boxes and climb again.",
            "Climb all three rungs on both boxes.",
            "Add depth with a higher arc and a longer follow-through, never by swinging harder. Long serves are the ladder's only real enemy.",
            UsapaBasics),
        D(88, "Lob Chase and Recover", "Movement", 2.5m, 10, Solo, Bucket,
            "Practice turning and running back for a lob, then recovering forward.",
            "Stand at the kitchen line with a bucket of balls and open court behind you.",
            "Toss a ball high over your own head toward the baseline, turn sideways, run it down with crossover steps, let it bounce, and hit a high defensive lob back over the net. Then jog back to the kitchen line. 15 reps.",
            "12 of 15 recovered balls landing in the far court.",
            "Turn and run first, never backpedal. Get behind the ball so you can hit it in front of you.",
            UsapaBasics),

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 3.0 — Beginner. The classic drilling partner tier, now with a full
        // solo set so a player with a wall can keep progressing.
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        D(1, "Cross-Court Dink Rally", "Dinking", 3.0m, 10, Partner, null,
            "Sustain a long cross-court dink rally with soft hands and a ball that stays below net height.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line on the same diagonal, continental grip, paddle out in front. One ball, spares nearby.",
            "Dink cross-court cooperatively and count out loud. Anything that pops up above net height is caught and the count restarts. Reach the target on the forehand diagonal, then switch to the backhand diagonal.",
            "50 consecutive dinks on each diagonal.",
            "Bend from the knees to get under the ball and lift with the shoulder. Keep the apex of every dink on your own side of the net.",
            UsapaBasics),
        D(2, "Serve and Stay In", "Serving", 3.0m, 10, Partner, Bucket,
            "Link a deep serve to a controlled third shot instead of treating the serve as the end of your job.",
            "Server behind the baseline with a bucket of balls; returner behind the far baseline.",
            "Serve deep into the back third of the box. The returner returns deep; the server stays back, lets it bounce, and hits a controlled third shot (drop or soft drive) into the far court. 20 sequences, then swap roles.",
            "14 of 20 serves deep and 14 of 20 third shots landing in.",
            "Consistency over power on both shots. After the serve, immediately get your paddle up and your weight balanced for the third ball.",
            UsapaBasics),
        D(3, "Kitchen Line Volley Exchange", "Volleys", 3.0m, 10, Partner, null,
            "Exchange slow, controlled volleys at the kitchen line with zero errors.",
            "Both players at their kitchen lines, paddles up, one ball.",
            "Volley back and forth at a slow, cooperative pace, counting each exchange. Return to ready position after every shot. Restart from zero on any error or pop-up.",
            "30 consecutive error-free exchanges.",
            "Paddle up and out in front between every hit. If the pace creeps up, soften your grip and block instead of punching.",
            UsapaBasics),
        D(4, "Return of Serve Deep Target", "Returns", 3.0m, 10, Partner, Bucket + ", " + Cones,
            "Drive deep returns to the server's backhand corner and follow them to the kitchen.",
            "Server with a bucket of balls; returner behind the far baseline. Mark the back third of the server's backhand corner with a cone or towel.",
            "Server feeds consistent serves. Returner drives each return deep to the marked corner and advances toward the kitchen line. 10 returns, then swap roles.",
            "8 of 10 returns landing in the back third.",
            "Hit through the ball with a long finish toward the target and start moving forward as soon as you have struck it.",
            UsapaBasics),
        D(41, "Third Shot Drop Introduction", "Drops", 3.0m, 10, Partner, Bucket,
            "Learn the third shot drop from mid-court first, then back up toward the baseline.",
            "Dropper at mid-court; the other player at the far kitchen line with a bucket of balls to feed deep returns.",
            "A deep return is fed to the dropper, who lets it bounce and hits a drop that lands softly in the kitchen, then steps in. Once 6 of 10 land, the dropper moves back toward the baseline and repeats. Swap roles.",
            "6 of 10 drops landing in the kitchen from mid-court, then from the baseline.",
            "Starting closer shortens the distance and builds confidence before the full baseline version. Lift from the shoulder, finish toward the kitchen.",
            UsapaBasics),
        D(42, "Volley Depth Control", "Volleys", 3.0m, 10, Partner, Bucket,
            "Control where a volley goes: block it short or push it deep, on command.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line; one feeds medium-pace volleys from a bucket.",
            "The feeder calls 'short' or 'deep' before each feed. On 'short', block the ball softly into the kitchen; on 'deep', push it at the feeder's feet or past them. 3 sets of 20, then swap roles.",
            "15 of 20 volleys landing where called in each set.",
            "Short comes from a softer grip and a stiller paddle; deep comes from a firmer grip and a longer push. Controlling a volley, not just returning it, turns the kitchen line from survival into offense.",
            UsapaBasics),
        D(43, "Return and Advance", "Returns", 3.0m, 10, Partner, Bucket,
            "Weld the deep return and the approach to the kitchen into one motion.",
            "Server behind the baseline with a bucket of balls; returner behind the far baseline.",
            "Serve; the returner returns deep and immediately advances toward the kitchen line, split-stepping as the third shot is struck. Play out the point from there. 15 reps per side, then swap roles.",
            "Returner established at the kitchen line before the third shot on 10 of 15 points.",
            "The return itself matters less than what follows it. Hit, move, split-step; this is the pattern for every return you will ever hit.",
            UsapaBasics),
        D(44, "Defensive Lob Basics", "Lobs", 3.0m, 10, Partner, Bucket,
            "Turn a losing mid-court position into a neutral one with a high defensive lob.",
            "Defender at mid-court; the other player at the kitchen line with a bucket of balls.",
            "The kitchen player drives balls at the defender, who throws up a high lob deep enough to push them off the line, then recovers position. 3 sets of 10, then swap roles.",
            "7 of 10 lobs landing past mid-court with enough height to force a retreat.",
            "A good defensive lob is not a winner; it is a rescue. Open the face early and lift high, then use the time to get your feet set.",
            UsapaBasics),
        D(45, "Mid-Court Reset Feeds", "Resets", 3.0m, 10, Partner, Bucket,
            "Absorb medium-pace balls in the transition zone and float them into the kitchen.",
            "Resetter in the transition zone; the other player at the kitchen line with a bucket of balls.",
            "The kitchen player hits medium-pace balls at the resetter's feet and body. The resetter absorbs each one with soft hands and floats it into the kitchen, advancing a half-step whenever a reset lands well. 3 sets of 12, then swap roles.",
            "8 of 12 resets landing in the kitchen per set.",
            "Getting stuck in mid-court is where beginners bleed points. Soft grip, short backswing, and let the ball's pace do the work.",
            UsapaBasics),
        D(46, "High Ball Put-Away Volleys", "Attacking", 3.0m, 10, Partner, Bucket,
            "Recognize an attackable ball and actually attack it instead of dinking it back.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line; one feeds floaters from a bucket that arrive above net height.",
            "The attacker punches each floater down into the open court or at the feeder's feet (with a warning). Anything below net height gets dinked back instead. 3 sets of 10 per side, then swap roles.",
            "8 of 10 high balls put away in each set, with no attacks on low balls.",
            "Anything below the net gets dinked; anything above gets hit. The decision is the drill; the paddle just follows.",
            UsapaBasics),
        D(47, "Shuffle and Dink Along the Line", "Movement", 3.0m, 10, Partner, null,
            "Keep a dink rally alive while both players reposition along the kitchen line.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line, one ball, spares nearby.",
            "Dink cross-court while both players side-shuffle one position — middle, wide, then back — after every two dinks, keeping the rally going while moving. 5-minute sets.",
            "Two 5-minute sets with fewer than five dropped rallies each.",
            "Feet first, paddle second. Dinking from a stable base is easy; dinking while repositioning is the game.",
            UsapaBasics),
        D(48, "Skinny Singles Rally Game", "General", 3.0m, 20, Partner, null,
            "Half-court singles that puts every skill in play with half the ground to cover.",
            "One player on each side using only one half of the court (both on the same diagonal or the same side). Keep score to 7.",
            "Serve, return and play out points inside the half court. Switch halves after each game so both diagonals get work.",
            "Two games to 7, one on each half.",
            "Every skill appears — serve, return, third shot, dinks, volleys — and rallies last long enough to actually practice them. Play patient points.",
            UsapaBasics),
        D(89, "Wall Dink Consistency 50", "Dinking", 3.0m, 10, Solo, Wall,
            "Extend your wall dink rally to 50 with a steadier arc and a quieter wrist.",
            "Mark net height (34 inches) on a solid wall. Stand 7 to 8 feet back in ready position.",
            "Dink into the wall above the line, let the rebound bounce once, and dink again. Count out loud; a ball below the line or a double bounce resets the count. Do forehand only, then backhand only, then alternating.",
            "50 in a row on each face, 30 alternating.",
            "Hold the finish for a beat after each dink and watch the ball land before you move. Rushing the next shot is what breaks long rallies.",
            KitchenDinking),
        D(90, "Third Shot Drop Self-Feed From the Baseline", "Drops", 3.0m, 10, Solo, Bucket,
            "Groove the full-length third shot drop with no pressure and plenty of repetitions.",
            "Bucket of balls behind the baseline. Picture two opponents at the far kitchen line.",
            "Drop a ball, let it bounce, and hit a drop that peaks on your side and lands in the kitchen. Hit 10 from the center, 10 from the forehand corner, 10 from the backhand corner.",
            "6 of 10 landing in the kitchen from each position.",
            "Lift with the shoulder and legs, not the wrist. Misses that land short are better misses than balls that sit up above the net.",
            KitchenThirdShot),
        D(91, "Wall Volley Reflex Builder", "Volleys", 3.0m, 10, Solo, Wall,
            "Shorten your reaction time by volleying off a wall from closer and closer.",
            "Stand 8 feet from a solid wall with your paddle up.",
            "Volley continuously without a bounce for 20 hits, then step one foot closer and do 20 more. Keep stepping in until you can no longer control 20 in a row, then step back out and finish with 20 clean ones.",
            "20 controlled volleys from 5 feet or closer.",
            "As you get closer, shrink the stroke: a block, not a punch. Paddle up and out front, elbow slightly bent, wrist firm.",
            MaxDrills),
        D(92, "Serve Depth and Direction Grid", "Serving", 3.0m, 10, Solo, Bucket + ", " + Cones,
            "Hit a nine-box serving grid so depth and direction become choices, not accidents.",
            "Divide one service box into a three-by-three grid with cones or chalk: wide, middle and T across; short, mid and deep down. Bucket of balls behind the baseline.",
            "Call a box, serve to it, and score a point when you hit it. Work through all nine boxes with three serves each, then switch to the other service box and repeat.",
            "14 of 27 called boxes hit on each side.",
            "Change direction with your shoulder alignment and depth with the arc. Keep the swing itself identical so the serve stays reliable under pressure.",
            MaxDrills),
        D(93, "Wall Return and Approach", "Returns", 3.0m, 10, Solo, Wall,
            "Combine a deep return off the wall with the footwork to follow it in.",
            "Stand 20 feet from a solid wall with a line marked at net height. Mark a kitchen line about 7 feet from the wall with tape or a cone.",
            "Hit a firm ball into the wall so the rebound arrives like a serve, let it bounce, return it high above the line, then advance toward your kitchen mark with a split-step as the ball comes off the wall. Catch the ball, walk back, repeat. 20 reps.",
            "16 of 20 returns above the line with a split-step before you reach the kitchen mark.",
            "Return first, then move; never move while you are still swinging. Arrive balanced with your paddle up.",
            KitchenTransition),
        D(94, "Reset off the Wall From Mid-Court", "Resets", 3.0m, 10, Solo, Wall,
            "Take pace off a hard ball and float it to a soft target, the core of every transition-zone reset.",
            "Stand 12 to 15 feet from a solid wall. Mark net height on the wall and a strip just above it as your target.",
            "Drive a ball hard into the wall, then take the fast rebound out of the air or on the short hop with a loose grip and send it softly back so it lands in the target strip. 3 sets of 10.",
            "7 of 10 resets hitting the soft target strip in each set.",
            "The block is a catch that happens to send the ball back. Shorten the backswing to nothing and let the paddle give at contact.",
            ThirdShotReset),
        D(95, "Put-Away Self-Toss", "Attacking", 3.0m, 10, Solo, Bucket + ", " + Cones,
            "Practice finishing high balls to targets so put-aways go where you aim, not just hard.",
            "Stand one step behind the kitchen line with a bucket of balls. Place a cone on each sideline of the far kitchen and one at the far baseline center.",
            "Toss a ball so it arrives above net height in front of you and volley it down at a called cone. 10 at each target, calling the cone before the toss.",
            "6 of 10 within a paddle's length of each cone.",
            "Contact out in front, paddle face slightly closed, and finish toward the target. Angle beats pace on a put-away.",
            MaxSpeedUp),
        D(96, "Ball Machine Transition Footwork", "Movement", 3.0m, 15, Solo, Machine,
            "Advance from the baseline to the kitchen against a steady stream of balls.",
            "Set a ball machine at the far kitchen line feeding medium-pace balls to mid-court every 3 to 4 seconds.",
            "Start at the baseline. Return the first ball, split-step, advance, and keep hitting soft shots into the kitchen until you reach the line. Walk back and repeat. 10 approaches.",
            "Reach the kitchen line on 7 of 10 approaches with every shot landing in the kitchen.",
            "Advance only behind a ball you have made soft. If a shot sits up, hold your ground for the next one instead of running in.",
            KitchenTransition),

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 3.5 — Intermediate. Third shot drops, kitchen game, transition
        // zone, with solo versions of each built around a wall or machine.
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        D(5, "Third Shot Drop From Baseline", "Drops", 3.5m, 10, Partner, Bucket,
            "Hit the full third shot drop from the baseline into the kitchen under a live eye.",
            "Dropper behind the baseline with a bucket of balls; the other player at the far kitchen line.",
            "The dropper drop-hits or is fed a return, then hits a drop into the kitchen. The kitchen player catches it or lets it bounce and calls whether it was attackable. 3 sets of 10 per side, then swap roles.",
            "7 of 10 unattackable drops in each set.",
            "A low, soft arc that peaks before the net and dies in the kitchen. If you miss, miss into the net rather than high; the high miss is the one that gets attacked.",
            KitchenThirdShot),
        D(6, "Transition Zone Drop and Advance", "Drops", 3.5m, 15, Partner, Bucket,
            "Drop from mid-court, advance behind it, and time your split-step to the reply.",
            "One player at mid-court in the transition zone; the other at the far kitchen line with a bucket of balls.",
            "The kitchen player feeds a ball to mid-court. Hit a drop into the kitchen, then advance toward the kitchen line, split-stepping as the kitchen player resets the ball back. Play two or three more balls, then reset. 3 sets of 10 sequences, then swap roles.",
            "Reach the kitchen line without popping a ball up on 7 of 10 sequences.",
            "Move the moment you have hit a good drop; stay put if it is high. Split-step as the reply is struck, not after.",
            KitchenTransition),
        D(7, "Dink Patterns: Straight and Cross", "Dinking", 3.5m, 10, Partner, null,
            "Alternate straight and cross-court dinks in a fixed pattern to build placement awareness.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line, one ball, spares nearby.",
            "Dink in a set pattern: two straight ahead, two cross-court, repeating, both players following the same pattern. Run 5-minute sets, then reverse the pattern.",
            "Two 5-minute sets holding the pattern with fewer than five breakdowns.",
            "Call the direction out loud before each dink until it becomes automatic. Changing direction with the paddle face, not a bigger swing, keeps the ball low.",
            KitchenDinking),
        D(8, "Backhand Reset Under Pressure", "Resets", 3.5m, 10, Partner, Bucket,
            "Absorb speed-ups aimed at your backhand and drop them softly back into the kitchen.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line; one feeds speed-ups at the other's backhand from a bucket.",
            "The attacker speeds up each ball at the resetter's backhand. The resetter gives with the paddle on contact and drops the ball softly into the kitchen. 3 sets of 15, then swap roles.",
            "10 of 15 resets landing unattackable in each set.",
            "Give with the paddle at contact and keep the face slightly open. Under pressure the backswing is your enemy; the block is your friend.",
            ThirdShotReset),
        D(49, "Aggressive Dink Targeting", "Dinking", 3.5m, 10, Partner, null,
            "Turn dinking from rally-survival into pressure-building by aiming every dink at a purpose.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line, one ball, spares nearby.",
            "Rally cooperatively but with intent: aim each dink at the backhand hip, the feet, or a short angle off the sideline, and your partner does the same. If a dink sits up, catch it and note who forced the error. 5-minute sets.",
            "Two 5-minute sets where most pop-ups are forced by placement, not by your own error.",
            "Pick the target before the ball arrives. Pressure comes from where the dink lands, not how hard it is hit.",
            KitchenDinking),
        D(50, "Drop vs Drive Decision Drill", "Drops", 3.5m, 15, Partner, Bucket,
            "Read the depth of the return and choose drop or drive before contact.",
            "Hitter behind the baseline with a bucket of balls; the other player at the far baseline returning feeds with varying depth.",
            "Feed a ball; the returner sends it back short or deep at random. Call 'drive' on short mid-court returns and hit at their body; call 'drop' on deep returns and drop into the kitchen. 3 sets of 15, then swap roles.",
            "12 of 15 correct calls per set with the shot landing in.",
            "The third shot is a decision, not a habit. Say the call out loud before contact so you can hear when you are guessing.",
            KitchenThirdShot),
        D(51, "Spin Serve Development", "Serving", 3.5m, 10, Solo, Bucket + ", " + Cones,
            "Add topspin to your serve so it kicks and stays deep.",
            "Bucket of balls behind the baseline. Mark the back third of the backhand side of each service box with a cone.",
            "Brush low to high through contact to add topspin, aiming deep to the backhand corner. 10 topspin serves per box, then 10 flat power serves per box for contrast, tracking depth and makes.",
            "7 of 10 topspin serves deep in each box.",
            "Accelerate up the back of the ball with a relaxed wrist. A serve that kicks and stays deep sets up weak returns and third shots that do not have to be perfect.",
            KitchenDinking),
        D(52, "Slice Return and Rush", "Returns", 3.5m, 10, Partner, Bucket,
            "Hit a low skidding slice return and use its slower flight to arrive set at the kitchen.",
            "Server with a bucket of balls; returner behind the far baseline.",
            "Serve; the returner slices the return low and deep to the backhand side and follows it to the kitchen line. Play out the point against the third shot. 15 reps per side, then swap roles.",
            "Established at the line before the third shot on 10 of 15 points.",
            "High-to-low swing path with an open face, finishing toward the target. The slice buys time; use it to move, not to admire the shot.",
            KitchenTransition),
        D(53, "Offensive Lob off the Dink", "Lobs", 3.5m, 10, Partner, null,
            "Disguise an offensive lob inside a dink rally so it lands behind your partner.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line, one ball, spares nearby.",
            "Dink cross-court. Every 4 to 6 dinks, the lobber disguises a lob: same low posture and backswing as a dink, then lifts over the backhand shoulder to land in the back quarter. The other player tracks it down and the point plays out. 3 sets of 10 lob attempts, then swap roles.",
            "6 of 10 lobs landing in the back quarter without being smashed.",
            "The disguise matters more than the height; a telegraphed lob is an overhead waiting to happen. Same setup as a dink until the last instant.",
            KitchenDinking),
        D(54, "Transition Zone Reset Ladder", "Resets", 3.5m, 15, Partner, Bucket,
            "Earn your way forward through the transition zone one unattackable reset at a time.",
            "Resetter starts near the baseline; the other player attacks from the kitchen line with a bucket of balls. Mark three stations: just behind the kitchen, mid transition zone, near the baseline.",
            "The attacker hits firm balls at the resetter. Three successful resets (soft, unattackable, in the kitchen) at a station moves the resetter forward one station; an attackable reset sends them back one. Climb the ladder twice, then swap roles.",
            "Two full climbs to the kitchen line.",
            "The deeper you are, the more swing discipline you need: shorter backswing, softer grip, higher margin over the net.",
            ThirdShotReset),
        D(55, "Speed-Up Selection Drill", "Attacking", 3.5m, 15, Partner, null,
            "Make shot selection the scoreboard: attack only balls above net height.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line, one ball. Keep score to 11.",
            "Dink rally where either player may speed up only a ball that arrives above net height; attacking anything lower loses the point instantly. The other player counters or resets and play continues.",
            "Two games to 11 with no illegal attacks.",
            "Most players at this level lose points attacking unattackable balls. If the ball is below the net, it is a dink, every time.",
            MaxSpeedUp),
        D(56, "Drop and Advance Footwork Patterns", "Movement", 3.5m, 10, Partner, Bucket,
            "Train feet that trust the drop: hit it, move behind it, and read the reply.",
            "Hitter behind the baseline with a bucket of balls; the other player at the far kitchen line.",
            "Hit a third shot drop, then advance two or three quick steps and split-step as the kitchen player strikes their reply. If it is a dink, continue in; if it is an attack, hold and reset. The kitchen player varies the response. 3 sets of 10 advances, then swap roles.",
            "Correct read (continue or hold) on 8 of 10 advances per set.",
            "Watching your drop instead of moving behind it is the classic stall at this level. Hit, move, split, read.",
            KitchenTransition),
        D(97, "Wall Volley Speed Ladder", "Volleys", 3.5m, 10, Solo, Wall,
            "Build fast, controlled hands by stepping down a wall-volley ladder from slow to quick.",
            "Mark net height on a solid wall. Start 8 feet back, paddle up.",
            "Volley above the line for 20 hits at a slow pace, then 20 at medium, then 20 as fast as you can control, all without a bounce. Drop a rung if you lose control, then climb again. 3 climbs.",
            "Three full climbs with 20 controlled fast volleys at the top of each.",
            "Speed comes from a shorter stroke, not a faster arm. At the fast rung the paddle barely moves back at all.",
            MaxDrills),
        D(98, "Self-Feed Drop Target Strip", "Drops", 3.5m, 10, Solo, Bucket + ", " + Cones,
            "Tighten your third shot drop from 'in the kitchen' to a specific landing strip.",
            "Lay a paddle-width strip of cones or a towel across the far kitchen about three feet from the net. Bucket of balls behind the baseline.",
            "Drop-hit third shots aiming to land in the strip. Hit 10 from the center, 10 from each corner, scoring a point for the strip and half a point for anywhere else in the kitchen.",
            "15 points out of 30 attempts.",
            "Control depth with the height of the arc: a higher peak lands shorter. Keep the swing speed the same and adjust only the face.",
            KitchenThirdShot),
        D(99, "Ball Machine Transition Resets", "Resets", 3.5m, 15, Solo, Machine,
            "Reset a stream of firm balls from the transition zone until the soft block is automatic.",
            "Set a ball machine at the far kitchen line feeding firm balls to mid-court at your feet and body, every 3 seconds.",
            "Stand in the transition zone. Reset each ball softly into the kitchen with a short block and a loose grip. 3 sets of 15 balls, moving one step closer to the kitchen each set.",
            "10 of 15 resets landing in the kitchen in each set.",
            "Get low early and meet the ball out in front. The machine never slows down, so your preparation has to.",
            ThirdShotReset),
        D(100, "Wall Dink Placement Boxes", "Dinking", 3.5m, 10, Solo, Wall + ", " + Cones,
            "Dink to targets on a wall so placement becomes a choice inside a steady rally.",
            "Mark net height on a solid wall and tape two boxes above the line, each a paddle-width wide, one to your left and one to your right. Stand 7 to 8 feet back.",
            "Keep a dink rally going off the wall, letting each rebound bounce once, and alternate hitting the left box and the right box. Score a point per box hit. 3 sets of 20 dinks.",
            "12 box hits per set of 20.",
            "Change direction by turning the paddle face at contact, not by swinging across the ball. Placement in a dink rally is what builds pressure.",
            KitchenDinking),
        D(101, "Serve and Shadow Third Shot", "General", 3.5m, 15, Solo, Bucket,
            "Rehearse the serve-team pattern alone: serve, recover, and hit a third shot from a self-feed.",
            "Bucket of balls behind the baseline with a few balls in your pocket.",
            "Serve, then immediately recover to ready position behind the baseline as if a return is coming. Drop a ball, let it bounce, and hit a third shot drop into the kitchen, then advance to mid-court with a split-step. Walk back and repeat. 15 sequences.",
            "12 of 15 serves in and 10 of 15 drops in the kitchen.",
            "Recover before you feed yourself; the point of the drill is the transition between shots, not the shots alone.",
            KitchenAdvancedThird),
        D(102, "Deep Return Wall Pressure", "Returns", 3.5m, 10, Solo, Wall + ", " + Cones,
            "Return a faster wall feed deep to a target under self-imposed scoring pressure.",
            "Stand 20 feet from a solid wall with net height marked. Mark a target band two feet above the line to represent a deep return.",
            "Drive a ball hard into the wall so it arrives like a strong serve, let it bounce, and return it into the target band. Score plus one for the band, minus one for below the line. 20 returns.",
            "Finish the set of 20 with a positive score.",
            "Against pace, shorten the backswing and lengthen the follow-through. A deep return hit at 70 percent beats a hard one that lands mid-court.",
            KitchenTransition),
        D(103, "Offensive Lob Disguise Self-Feed", "Lobs", 3.5m, 10, Solo, Bucket + ", " + Cones,
            "Build a lob that looks like a dink until the last instant.",
            "Stand at the kitchen line with a bucket of balls. Mark the back quarter of the far court with cones.",
            "Drop a ball at your feet and hit two dinks into the far kitchen, then on the third self-feed keep the same posture and backswing and lift a lob into the marked back quarter. 15 sequences.",
            "10 of 15 lobs landing in the back quarter from a dink-identical setup.",
            "Nothing changes until contact: same knee bend, same backswing. The lift comes from opening the face and finishing high.",
            KitchenDinking),
        D(104, "Attack or Dink Wall Decision", "Attacking", 3.5m, 10, Solo, Wall,
            "Train the attack-versus-dink decision on every ball using a wall and a net-height line.",
            "Mark net height on a solid wall and a second line one foot above it. Stand 8 feet back, paddle up.",
            "Rally off the wall. Any rebound you can contact above the higher line, speed up firmly; anything lower, dink softly above the net line. Say 'attack' or 'dink' before each hit. 3 sets of 30 balls.",
            "3 sets with no attacks on low balls and at least 10 speed-ups per set.",
            "The decision is made as the ball rises, not at contact. Low ball, soft hands; high ball, firm hands and down.",
            MaxSpeedUp),

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 4.0 — Advanced. Competitive patterns, speed-ups and resets, with
        // solo hands work off the wall and machine and one doubles group drill.
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        D(9, "Speed-Up Attack and Counter", "Attacking", 4.0m, 10, Partner, null,
            "Trade speed-ups and counters at the kitchen line to sharpen body-ball reflexes.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line, one ball, spares nearby.",
            "Player A speeds up a ball at Player B's body; Player B counters back at Player A's feet. The rally continues at pace until someone misses or resets, then restart with roles swapped. 5 minutes of continuous play.",
            "5 minutes with most exchanges lasting three or more fast balls.",
            "Counter at the feet, not the body, so the attacker has to hit up. Paddle in front, elbow bent, and let the grip stay relaxed until contact.",
            MaxSpeedUp),
        D(10, "Erne Setup and Execution", "Volleys", 4.0m, 15, Partner, Bucket,
            "Set up and execute the Erne: pull the opponent wide, then volley from outside the kitchen.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line; the other player has a bucket to feed wide dinks on command. Clear space beside the kitchen corner.",
            "Shadow the footwork 10 times: dink cross-court, read the wide reply, step through the kitchen corner or around the post and volley from outside the non-volley zone, landing beyond the kitchen. Then run 15 live reps off wide feeds, then swap roles.",
            "10 clean shadow reps and 10 of 15 live Ernes landing in.",
            "Commit early and go all the way: a half-Erne is a kitchen fault. Your feet must be outside the zone before contact.",
            MaxErne),
        D(11, "Advanced Third Shot Drop Sequences", "Drops", 4.0m, 15, Partner, Bucket,
            "Keep dropping under live pressure until one is unattackable, then earn the advance.",
            "Dropper behind the baseline with a bucket of balls; the other player at the far kitchen line applying pressure.",
            "The dropper hits third shot drops; the kitchen player speeds up anything above the net and dinks anything below. The dropper advances only after an unattackable drop, then the point plays out. Score points, 3 sets of 10 sequences, then swap roles.",
            "Advance within two drops on 7 of 10 sequences.",
            "Under pressure the margin shrinks: aim for the kitchen line rather than the net cord. The quality of the drop, not the speed of the advance, wins the sequence.",
            KitchenAdvancedThird),
        D(12, "Competitive Dinking With Speed-Up Option", "Dinking", 4.0m, 15, Partner, null,
            "Dink cross-court with the speed-up live at all times, and play points to 11.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line on the same diagonal. Keep score to 11.",
            "Rally cross-court dinks. Either player may speed up at any time; the other must counter or reset. Play points to 11, then switch diagonals.",
            "Two games to 11, one on each diagonal.",
            "Patience creates the opening and recognition takes it. Speed up only balls you can hit down; the rest are patience.",
            ThirdShotDinking),
        D(57, "Fifth Shot Drop Consistency", "Drops", 4.0m, 15, Partner, Bucket,
            "Isolate the fifth shot: drive the third, follow in, and drop the next ball from the transition zone.",
            "Hitter behind the baseline with a bucket of balls; the other player at the far kitchen line.",
            "Drive the third shot at the kitchen player, who blocks it back to mid-court. Follow in and hit a fifth shot drop from the transition zone, then finish the advance and play out the point. 3 sets of 10 sequences, then swap roles.",
            "7 of 10 fifth shot drops landing unattackable per set.",
            "The drive only works if the fifth ball lands soft. Slow your feet as you reach the drop so you hit it balanced instead of on the run.",
            KitchenAdvancedThird),
        D(58, "Punch Volley Target Battle", "Volleys", 4.0m, 10, Partner, Cones,
            "Win firefights on precision: punch volleys at targets at the feet and hips.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line. Place cone targets at each other's feet and open hips. Keep score to 11.",
            "Exchange firm punch volleys aiming for the targets. A cone hit or a forced pop-up scores a point. First to 11.",
            "Two games to 11.",
            "Directed volleys at the feet and hips win firefights before they start. Aim with the paddle face, not with a bigger swing.",
            MaxSpeedUp),
        D(59, "Pressure Serve Percentages", "Serving", 4.0m, 10, Solo, Bucket + ", " + Cones,
            "Serve at full match pace to called targets with a score that punishes faults.",
            "Mark wide, body and T targets in the back third of each service box. Bucket of balls behind the baseline.",
            "Hit 20 serves at full pace, calling a target before each. Score plus one for a called-target make in the back third, minus two for any fault. Finish positive or repeat the set.",
            "A positive score over 20 serves on both sides.",
            "At this level the serve is a weapon with a cost. Pick a pace you can repeat at plus one rather than one that produces minus two.",
            MaxSpeedUp),
        D(60, "Deep Slice Return Under Pressure", "Returns", 4.0m, 10, Partner, Bucket,
            "Return full-power serves low and deep to a called corner and beat the third shot to the line.",
            "Server with a bucket of balls serving at full power with mixed placement and spin; returner behind the far baseline.",
            "The returner slices every return low and deep to a called corner and gets to the kitchen line before the third shot. Track the make percentage over 20 returns; anything landing mid-court counts as a miss even if it is in. Swap roles.",
            "14 of 20 returns deep in the called corner.",
            "Against big servers, the skidding return that stays low and deep keeps the serving team off the drive. Shorten the backswing, lengthen the finish.",
            ThirdShotDinking),
        D(61, "Lob and Overhead Battle", "Lobs", 4.0m, 15, Partner, null,
            "Play both halves of the lob exchange: the disguised lob going up and the overhead footwork coming down.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line. Keep score to 11 and rotate roles every 5 points.",
            "One player throws up offensive lobs from the kitchen line; the other tracks back and answers with an overhead or a controlled reset lob. Play out every point.",
            "Two games to 11 with roles rotated.",
            "Going up: disguise and placement over the backhand shoulder. Coming down: drop step, crossover, never backpedal.",
            MaxErne),
        D(62, "Firefight Reset Escape", "Resets", 4.0m, 10, Partner, null,
            "Learn to turn the temperature down: kill a firefight with a dead-hands reset.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line. Keep score to 7.",
            "Start a kitchen-line firefight at full speed. At the first viable ball, kill the pace with a dead-hands reset that drops into the kitchen and forces the rally back to dinks. Score a point for each successful de-escalation, lose one for feeding an attackable ball.",
            "Two games to 7.",
            "Elite hands are not just fast; they know how to slow the exchange down. Loosen the grip and let the paddle absorb rather than block.",
            ThirdShotReset),
        D(63, "Two-Ball Speed-Up Combinations", "Attacking", 4.0m, 15, Partner, null,
            "Plan speed-ups as two-shot combinations: the attack and the finish.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line, one ball, spares nearby.",
            "Attack the first ball to a chosen target (backhand shoulder, hip or feet), anticipate the counter, and finish the second ball to the open court. The other player counters at full effort and every exchange is played out. 3 sets of 10 initiations per player.",
            "Win 6 of 10 initiated combinations per set.",
            "The first speed-up rarely wins at this level. Choose the finish before you hit the attack and be moving to it as the counter comes.",
            MaxSpeedUp),
        D(64, "Transition Zone Defense Footwork", "Movement", 4.0m, 10, Partner, Bucket,
            "Earn forward progress through the transition zone one shot at a time against nonstop drives.",
            "Defender at the baseline; the other player at the kitchen line with a bucket of balls.",
            "The kitchen player drives balls at the advancing defender without pause. The defender split-steps at each strike, absorbs what comes, advances only behind a ball made unattackable, and holds or retreats a half-step otherwise. 3 sets of 10 approaches, then swap roles.",
            "Reach the line without a pop-up on 6 of 10 approaches per set.",
            "Forward progress is earned per shot, not assumed. The split-step is non-negotiable even when the ball is already on its way.",
            KitchenTransition),
        D(105, "Ball Machine Fifth Shot Drops", "Drops", 4.0m, 15, Solo, Machine,
            "Repeat the fifth shot drop from the transition zone against a machine that never gives you an easy ball.",
            "Set a ball machine at the far kitchen line feeding firm, low balls to mid-court every 3 to 4 seconds, with some side-to-side oscillation if available.",
            "Start one step inside the baseline. Take the first ball as a drive, move in behind it, and drop the next ball from the transition zone into the kitchen. Reset to the start after every two balls. 3 sets of 10 sequences.",
            "7 of 10 drops landing unattackable in each set.",
            "Settle your feet before the drop even if it costs you half a step. A balanced drop from mid-court beats a running drop from the kitchen line.",
            KitchenAdvancedThird),
        D(106, "Wall Firefight Hands", "Volleys", 4.0m, 10, Solo, Wall,
            "Simulate a kitchen-line firefight by volleying hard off a wall from close range.",
            "Mark net height on a solid wall. Stand 6 feet back, paddle up at chest height.",
            "Volley firmly above the line and keep the exchange going without a bounce at the fastest pace you can control. Alternate forehand and backhand as the rebound dictates. 5 rounds of 30 seconds, resting 30 seconds between.",
            "5 rounds with fewer than three lost balls each.",
            "Keep your paddle in front and your elbow bent so you can block from both sides. Speed lives in the forearm, not the shoulder.",
            MaxSpeedUp),
        D(107, "Topspin Dink Wall Rolls", "Dinking", 4.0m, 10, Solo, Wall,
            "Add a rolling topspin dink that dips at the opponent's feet instead of floating.",
            "Mark net height on a solid wall with a second line six inches above it. Stand 7 feet back.",
            "Dink off the wall, letting each rebound bounce, and brush up the back of the ball so it crosses the wall between the two lines and dips. Count only dinks that land between the lines. 3 sets of 25.",
            "15 of 25 between the lines in each set.",
            "The brush comes from the forearm rotating low to high while the wrist stays quiet. A floating dink gets attacked; a dipping dink gets reset.",
            ThirdShotDinking),
        D(108, "Serve Plus One Self-Feed Patterns", "General", 4.0m, 15, Solo, Bucket + ", " + Cones,
            "Rehearse serve-plus-one patterns alone: serve to a target, then play the planned third shot.",
            "Bucket of balls behind the baseline with a few in your pocket. Cones marking the wide, body and T targets in each box and a strip across the far kitchen.",
            "Call a serve target and a third shot (drop or drive) before serving. Serve to the target, recover, drop a ball to yourself at the baseline, and hit the called third shot: drops into the kitchen strip, drives through the far mid-court. 20 sequences.",
            "14 of 20 serves on target and 14 of 20 third shots landing in.",
            "Choose the whole pattern before the serve so the third shot is a decision already made, not a reaction.",
            MaxSpeedUp),
        D(109, "Wall Counter-Attack Reflexes", "Attacking", 4.0m, 10, Solo, Wall,
            "Counter a speed-up with a controlled counter-attack to a target instead of a wild block.",
            "Mark net height on a solid wall and tape two paddle-sized targets a foot above the line, left and right. Stand 7 feet back.",
            "Hit a hard ball into the wall, then counter the fast rebound out of the air into a called target. Alternate targets. 3 sets of 15 counters.",
            "9 of 15 counters hitting the called target in each set.",
            "Keep the counter compact and redirect the ball's own pace. The target, not the power, is what wins the exchange.",
            MaxSpeedUp),
        D(110, "Reset Under Pace off the Wall", "Resets", 4.0m, 10, Solo, Wall,
            "Reset genuinely hard balls into a soft target, the hands you need to survive a 4.0 firefight.",
            "Mark net height on a solid wall and a soft target strip just above it. Stand 10 feet back.",
            "Drive a ball as hard as you can control into the wall, then absorb the rebound with a loose grip and drop it into the target strip. Alternate forehand and backhand sides. 3 sets of 12.",
            "8 of 12 resets in the target strip per set.",
            "Loosen the grip before the ball arrives, not at contact. The paddle gives an inch backward as the ball meets it.",
            ThirdShotReset),
        D(111, "Lob Recovery Footwork Circuit", "Movement", 4.0m, 10, Solo, Bucket + ", " + Cones,
            "Train the drop-step, crossover and recovery that make lobs survivable.",
            "Stand at the kitchen line with a bucket of balls. Cone at the baseline center and one at each kitchen corner.",
            "Toss a ball high over your own head toward the baseline. Drop step, turn, crossover-run to it, hit a controlled overhead or reset lob into the far court, then sprint back and touch the kitchen line. 3 sets of 8 reps.",
            "3 sets with every rep completed in under 10 seconds and the ball landing in.",
            "Never backpedal. The first step is a drop step with the foot on the ball's side, then run with crossover steps and set up behind the ball.",
            MaxErne),
        D(112, "Shake and Bake Pattern Drill", "Attacking", 4.0m, 20, Group, Bucket,
            "Run the shake and bake as a four-player pattern: drive, crash, poach, finish.",
            "Two teams of two in doubles positions. Serving team has a bucket of balls.",
            "The serving team hits a third shot drive while their partner crashes the net; the returning team blocks the drive and the crasher poaches the fifth ball for a put-away. Play out the point. Rotate all four positions every 5 sequences, 20 sequences total.",
            "Win 12 of 20 sequences as the serving team.",
            "Driver: hit through the ball at the kitchen player's hip. Crasher: move on the drive, not after it. Defenders: block soft to the driver's feet to take the poach away.",
            MaxSpeedUp),

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 5.0 — Professional. Tournament patterns and match simulation, with
        // the solo circuits a touring player runs between sessions.
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        D(13, "ATP and Roll Volley Combination", "Attacking", 5.0m, 15, Partner, Bucket,
            "Chain the Around-The-Post shot into a roll volley finish at tournament pace.",
            "Hitter at the kitchen line; the other player at the far kitchen line with a bucket of balls feeding wide dinks.",
            "The feed pulls the hitter wide. Hit the ATP around the net post, recover, and finish the next ball with a roll volley to the open court. 20 reps each side, then swap roles.",
            "12 of 20 ATPs in and the roll volley landing in behind them.",
            "Let the wide ball travel past the post before you swing and keep the follow-through low. The recovery step after the ATP is what makes the roll volley possible.",
            UsapaRules),
        D(14, "Full-Court Scenario Game Simulation", "General", 5.0m, 20, Partner, null,
            "Play full-pace points built around pattern play and analyze each one afterward.",
            "Singles or skinny singles, full pace. Best of 21.",
            "Points start with a serve and play out at full pace with an emphasis on patterns: third shot, advance, reset if needed, find and attack the first pop-up. Analyze the pattern after each point in one sentence.",
            "Best of 21 with a one-sentence pattern note after every point.",
            "Decide the pattern before the serve and judge the point on whether you ran it, not on who won it.",
            UsapaRules),
        D(15, "High-Level Firefight Training", "Volleys", 5.0m, 10, Partner, null,
            "Rapid-fire speed-up exchanges with resets forbidden: counter or win the point.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line. Keep score to 10 and track outcomes.",
            "Engage in continuous speed-up exchanges. No resets allowed; every ball must be countered or the point is lost. Play 10-point games and record who wins each exchange and how.",
            "Three 10-point games with full tracking.",
            "Fast hands come from a short stroke and a still head. Pick your counter target before the exchange starts.",
            UsapaRules),
        D(16, "Serve Plus Tournament Pattern Play", "Serving", 5.0m, 20, Partner, Bucket + ", " + Cones,
            "Serve to the T, body and wide targets, each followed by a planned third shot pattern.",
            "Server with a bucket of balls and cones marking three targets per box; the other player returns live.",
            "Call a serve target and a third shot plan (drop versus drive based on return depth) before each serve. Serve, read the return, execute the plan, and play out the point with scoring. 20 points per server, then swap.",
            "Serve on target 14 of 20 times and execute the planned third shot on 14 of 20.",
            "Full match-simulation pressure: a serve with no plan for the third ball is a wasted serve.",
            UsapaRules),
        D(65, "Pressure Dinking With Zone Penalties", "Dinking", 5.0m, 15, Partner, Cones,
            "Dink to called zones under a live attack threat, the way dinking exists at 5.0.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line. Mark zones in each kitchen: backhand side, sideline third, and at the feet. Keep score to 11.",
            "Each dink must land in the currently called zone; the caller changes the zone every few balls. Hitting the wrong zone or sitting one up loses the point outright, and either player may speed up a legitimately attackable ball at any time.",
            "Two games to 11.",
            "Precision under pressure means watching the ball onto the paddle while already knowing the zone. Never trade placement for safety.",
            UsapaRules),
        D(66, "Third and Fifth Shot Precision Circuit", "Drops", 5.0m, 20, Partner, Bucket + ", " + Cones,
            "A timed circuit of third shots, fifth shots and drops off the run with restart penalties.",
            "Mark a paddle-width target strip in the far kitchen. Dropper with a bucket of balls; the other player at the far kitchen line applying live pressure.",
            "Hit 10 third shot drops from the baseline into the strip, 10 fifth shot drops from the transition zone under live pressure, and 10 drops off the run from the sideline. Restart any set where fewer than 7 land unattackable. Run the full circuit twice, then swap roles.",
            "Two full circuits with every set at 7 of 10 or better.",
            "Tournament drops are hit from wherever the last shot left you, not from a stance. Set the paddle early so your feet can keep moving.",
            UsapaRules),
        D(67, "Return Depth Pinning Game", "Returns", 5.0m, 15, Partner, Cones,
            "Live points where every return must land in the back three feet or the returner is penalized.",
            "Mark a line three feet inside the server's baseline. Keep score to 11.",
            "Play live points. Any return landing short of the marked band restarts the point and costs the returner a point. The server plays full-speed drives or drops off anything short.",
            "Two games to 11 as returner.",
            "A mid-court return is a lost rally at this level. Give the return more height and hit through it with a full finish.",
            UsapaRules),
        D(68, "Tournament Lob and Cover Rotation", "Lobs", 5.0m, 15, Group, null,
            "Doubles pattern: the offensive lob, the switch, and the point played out at match pace.",
            "Two teams of two in doubles positions, all four at or near the kitchen line.",
            "The offensive lob goes up over one kitchen-line player's shoulder; that player's teammate tracks it down while the lobbed player switches sides, and the point plays out against a full-speed attack on the recovery ball. Rotate all four positions. 20 total sequences.",
            "20 sequences with a clear 'switch' call on every lob.",
            "Communication wins the lob exchange: call 'switch' before you move, and decide overhead-versus-reset in the air, not after the bounce.",
            UsapaRules),
        D(69, "Full-Speed Reset Gauntlet", "Resets", 5.0m, 15, Partner, Bucket,
            "Survive maximum-pace put-aways from mid-court until three consecutive resets earn the line.",
            "Defender at mid-court; the attacker at the kitchen line with a bucket of balls feeding rapid put-aways at the body and feet (two attackers if available).",
            "Reset each ball into the kitchen. Three consecutive unattackable resets earn the defender the kitchen line and the sequence restarts. Count gauntlet completions in 15 minutes, then swap roles.",
            "Five or more completions in 15 minutes.",
            "Defense at this level means neutralizing balls most players would not touch. Stay low, paddle out front, grip at a 3 out of 10.",
            UsapaRules),
        D(70, "Firefight Finish Patterns", "Attacking", 5.0m, 15, Partner, null,
            "Run scripted attack sequences at full speed until the pattern, not the reflex, wins the firefight.",
            "Both players at the kitchen line, one ball, spares nearby.",
            "Initiate with a speed-up to the backhand shoulder, counter the counter to the hip, and finish to the open court; then run the mirror pattern. The other player defends live, and any pattern ball that misses its target restarts the rep. 10 clean sequences per pattern, per player.",
            "10 clean sequences of each pattern for each player.",
            "Pros do not win firefights with reflexes alone; they win with pre-decided sequences. Know the third ball before you hit the first.",
            UsapaRules),
        D(71, "Court Coverage Scramble Simulation", "Movement", 5.0m, 15, Partner, Bucket,
            "Cover a brutal, rhythmless feed sequence with full recovery footwork between every shot.",
            "Mover starts at the baseline; the other player (or a coach) at the far kitchen line with a bucket of balls.",
            "The feed has no rhythm: deep drive, short drop, wide angle, lob, body speed-up. Play every ball with full recovery footwork between shots — split-step, push-off, recover to the tactically correct position, not just center. 3 sets of 2 minutes at match intensity, then swap roles.",
            "3 sets with every ball played and a recovery before the next feed.",
            "Tournament movement is defined by the worst ball you can still cover. Recover to where the next ball is likely, not to the middle.",
            UsapaRules),
        D(72, "Constraint Match Play", "General", 5.0m, 20, Partner, null,
            "Full-pace games with a rotating constraint that confiscates your favorite weapon.",
            "Singles or skinny singles at match pace. Games to 11.",
            "Each game adds one constraint: every third shot must be a drop; no drives allowed; all speed-ups must target the backhand; one player must take every possible ball out of the air. Rotate the constraint each game.",
            "Four games to 11, one per constraint.",
            "Constraints force pattern depth you would never choose voluntarily. Notice which tools hold up when the usual one is taken away.",
            UsapaRules),
        D(113, "Ball Machine Drops off the Run", "Drops", 5.0m, 15, Solo, Machine + ", " + Cones,
            "Hit unattackable drops from wherever the last shot left you, fed by a machine that moves you.",
            "Set a ball machine at the far kitchen line with full side-to-side oscillation feeding firm balls to the baseline and mid-court every 3 seconds. Mark a target strip in the far kitchen.",
            "Chase every ball and drop it into the target strip, recovering toward center between feeds. 3 sets of 15 balls, moving the start position from baseline to mid-court each set.",
            "10 of 15 drops in the strip in each set.",
            "Set the paddle face early and let the legs do the adjusting. Off the run, shorten the backswing and trust the lift.",
            KitchenAdvancedThird),
        D(114, "Wall Speed Hands Below the Line", "Volleys", 5.0m, 10, Solo, Wall,
            "Tournament-pace hands with a height constraint: every volley must stay in a tight band.",
            "Mark net height on a solid wall and a second line 20 inches above it. Stand 6 feet back, paddle up.",
            "Volley as fast as you can control, keeping every ball between the two lines without a bounce. A ball outside the band or a bounce ends the run. 5 runs, recording your longest.",
            "A run of 40 or more volleys inside the band.",
            "Pace with control comes from a firm wrist and a paddle that never drops below your chest. Breathe; tension slows the hands.",
            MaxSpeedUp),
        D(115, "Match-Pace Serve Target Percentages", "Serving", 5.0m, 15, Solo, Bucket + ", " + Cones,
            "Track serve percentages to tight targets at full pace, the way a tournament scorer would.",
            "Mark wide, body and T targets in the back two feet of each service box. Bucket of balls behind the baseline.",
            "Hit 30 serves at match pace, rotating targets and sides every serve and calling each before contact. Record makes on target, in-but-off-target, and faults.",
            "20 of 30 on target with no more than two faults.",
            "Build a pre-serve routine and run it identically every time. Percentages come from the routine, not from the arm.",
            UsapaRules),
        D(116, "ATP Shadow and Self-Feed", "Attacking", 5.0m, 15, Solo, Bucket,
            "Groove the Around-The-Post footwork and swing path without needing anyone to feed you wide.",
            "Bucket of balls at the kitchen line near a sideline. Clear space beyond the net post.",
            "Shadow the ATP 10 times: shuffle wide, let the imaginary ball travel past the post, swing low and flat around it. Then toss a ball wide and low beyond the sideline for yourself, let it bounce, and hit the ATP around the post into the far court. 20 live reps per side.",
            "12 of 20 ATPs landing in on each side.",
            "Patience is the shot: let the ball drift past the post before you swing, and keep the finish low so the ball travels flat around it.",
            UsapaRules),
        D(117, "Wall Dink Pressure With Zone Calls", "Dinking", 5.0m, 15, Solo, Wall + ", " + Cones,
            "Dink to called zones on the wall under a self-imposed speed-up threat.",
            "Mark net height on a solid wall and tape three zones above it: left, middle, right. Stand 7 feet back.",
            "Rally dinks off the wall, letting each rebound bounce, and call a zone before every dink. Any ball that rebounds above shoulder height you must speed up into the middle zone and then reset the rally with the next ball. 3 sets of 40 dinks.",
            "30 of 40 zone hits per set with every high ball attacked.",
            "Read the rebound off the wall the way you would read an opponent's paddle. The call comes before the ball reaches you, never after.",
            ThirdShotDinking),
        D(118, "Erne Footwork Repetitions", "Movement", 5.0m, 10, Solo, Cones,
            "Make the Erne footwork automatic with high-repetition shadow work along the kitchen corner.",
            "No ball needed. Stand at the kitchen line a step inside the sideline. Place a cone just outside the kitchen corner as your landing spot.",
            "Shadow a cross-court dink, read the imaginary wide reply, and either step through the kitchen corner to land on the cone or leap around the post, swinging a volley with both feet outside the zone, then recover to the kitchen line. 3 sets of 10 per side.",
            "3 sets with every rep landing outside the zone and recovering to the line within two seconds.",
            "The Erne is decided on the dink before it: go all the way or not at all. Land balanced with the paddle already in front.",
            MaxErne),
        D(119, "Ball Machine Reset Gauntlet", "Resets", 5.0m, 15, Solo, Machine,
            "Survive a machine set to maximum pace from mid-court, three unattackable resets at a time.",
            "Set a ball machine at the far kitchen line at its fastest controllable speed feeding at the body and feet in mid-court, every 2 to 3 seconds.",
            "Stand in the transition zone. Reset every ball into the kitchen; three consecutive unattackable resets earn you a step toward the line. Reach the line, walk back, repeat. Count completions in 15 minutes.",
            "Four or more completions in 15 minutes.",
            "Grip at a 3 out of 10 and paddle out in front before the ball leaves the machine. The machine's pace is the drill; your job is to make it disappear.",
            ThirdShotReset),
        D(120, "Overhead Series Self-Toss", "Lobs", 5.0m, 10, Solo, Bucket + ", " + Cones,
            "Finish overheads to targets from deep in the court after a full retreat.",
            "Bucket of balls at the kitchen line. Cones in the far court at both back corners and one just behind the far kitchen line.",
            "Toss a ball high and deep over your own head, drop step and retreat behind it, and hit an overhead to a called cone. Alternate a put-away to a back corner with a controlled angled overhead toward the short cone. 3 sets of 10.",
            "6 of 10 within a paddle's length of the called cone per set.",
            "Set up behind the ball so contact is slightly in front, and swing to the target rather than at the ball. Placement finishes points; pace alone comes back.",
            MaxErne),
    };
}
